content.thinking:
In the markdown test, body is '从 [[architecture]] 和 [[ghost]] 开始。' and outgoing is ['architecture', 'ghost'] — both sorted and original order coincide. So I can't distinguish from existing tests. The spec says "重复引用只出现一次并保持原有排序" — I read this as "duplicates appear once, keeping the existing order/behavior". Since the current behavior sorts, and "其余链接行为保持兼容" (other link behaviors remain compatible), I'll keep sorting. That's the safe choice — "保持原有排序" = preserve the original (current) ordering, which is sorted.

Now let me write the new link extractor. I'll replace `linksFrom` in workspace.js with a scanner.

Design:

```js
const LINK_TARGET = /[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*/giu;

function scanRegion(region) {
  // returns array of lowercased link targets found in a non-fence region
  const found = [];
  const len = region.length;
  let i = 0;
  while (i < len) {
    const ch = region[i];
    if (ch === '`') {
      let j = i;
      while (j < len && region[j] === '`') j += 1;
      const run = j - i;
      // find a closing run of exactly `run` backticks
      let k = j;
      let close = -1;
      while (k < len) {
        if (region[k] === '`') {
          let m = k;
          while (m < len && region[m] === '`') m += 1;
          if (m - k === run) { close = k; break; }
          k = m;
        } else {
          k += 1;
        }
      }
      if (close === -1) {
        // opening run is literal text; continue scanning after it
        i = j;
      } else {
        i = close + run;
      }
    } else if (ch === '[' && region[i + 1] === '[') {
      // count backslashes immediately before i
      let bs = 0;
      let p = i - 1;
      while (p >= 0 && region[p] === '\\') { bs += 1; p -= 1; }
      if (bs % 2 === 1) {
        // escaped: literal text, continue after the opening brackets
        i += 2;
        continue;
      }
      // match target then ]]
      let k = i + 2;
      LINK_TARGET.lastIndex = k;
      const match = LINK_TARGET.exec(region);
      if (match && match.index === k && region[k + match[0].length] === ']' && region[k + match[0].length + 1] === ']') {
        found.push(match[0].toLowerCase());
        i = k + match[0].length + 2;
      } else {
        i += 1;
      }
    } else {
      i += 1;
    }
  }
  return found;
}
```

Wait, there's an issue with the LINK_TARGET regex and lastIndex: `/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*/giu` — exec with lastIndex set to k. If match.index === k, then the target starts right after `[[`. Then check `]]` follows. Good. But careful: the regex could match a prefix and then `]]` check fails — e.g. `[[a b]]`: at k, regex matches `a`, then next char is ` ` not `]` → fail, i += 1. Then at i+1 (the `[`? no wait). Hmm, `[[a b]]`: i=0 `[`, i=1 `[`, k=2. regex matches `a` at index 2. region[3] = ' ' → fail. i becomes 1. At i=1: region[1]='[', region[2]='a' → not `[[`, i=2. ... eventually no match. Good.

But wait — there's a subtlety: what about `[[a.b]]`? regex matches `a.b` (greedy), then `]]` → match. Good. What about `[[a.]]`? regex matches `a.` (greedy: `[a-z0-9._-]*` matches `.`), then `]]` → match → target `a.`. Same as old regex. Good.

Now the fence splitting. I'll process line by line:

```js
function linksFrom(body) {
  const text = body.replace(/\r\n?/g, '\n');
  const lines = text.split('\n');
  const found = [];
  let inFence = false;
  let fenceChar = '';
  let fenceLen = 0;
  let regionStart = 0; // start index in text of current non-fence region
  let offset = 0; // char offset of current line start
  for (const line of lines) {
    const lineStart = offset;
    offset += line.length + 1; // +1 for the newline
    if (inFence) {
      // check closing fence
      const close = matchFence(line);
      if (close && close.char === fenceChar && close.len >= fenceLen) {
        inFence = false;
        // region resumes after this line
        regionStart = offset;
      }
    } else {
      const open = matchFence(line);
      if (open) {
        // scan region before fence
        found.push(...scanRegion(text.slice(regionStart, lineStart)));
        inFence = true;
        fenceChar = open.char;
        fenceLen = open.len;
      }
    }
  }
  if (!inFence) {
    found.push(...scanRegion(text.slice(regionStart)));
  }
  return [...new Set(found)];
}
```

Wait, but `matchFence` needs to handle the info string and the backtick-in-info restriction. Let me write:

```js
function matchFence(line) {
  const match = /^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)$/u.exec(line);
  if (!match) return null;
  const info = match[2];
  if (match[1][0] === '`' && info.includes('`')) return null;
  // closing fence: info must be only spaces/tabs
  return { char: match[1][0], len: match[1].length, info };
}
```

Hmm wait, for the closing fence, the spec says "后面只能有空格或制表符" (after it only spaces or tabs). So when checking a closing fence, the info part must match `/^[ \t]*$/`. For opening, info can be anything (language name). Let me distinguish:

```js
function fenceOf(line) {
  const match = /^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})(.*)$/u.exec(line);
  if (!match) return null;
  const info = match[2];
  if (match[1][0] === '`' && info.includes('`')) return null;
  return { char: match[1][0], len: match[1].length, info };
}
```

Then closing check: `close.info` must be `/^[ \t]*$/`.

Wait, but there's a subtlety with the backtick info rule: CommonMark says the info string for a backtick fence must not contain a backtick. My check `info.includes('`')` handles that. But also — what about a closing fence line like ` ``` ` with trailing spaces? info = ' ' → `/^[ \t]*$/` passes. Good.

Also, what about a line like ` ```` ` `` (4 backticks) closing a 3-backtick fence? len 4 >= 3, same char → closes. Good. And `~~~` can't close a ``` fence (different char). Good.

Edge: opening fence with 4 backticks, closing with 3 backticks → len 3 < 4 → doesn't close. Good.

Now, one more consideration: the opening fence regex `^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})` — note that `~{3,}` and `` `{3,} ``. A line ` ```` ` `` (4 backticks) matches with len 4. Good.

But wait — what about a line that starts with 0-3 spaces then backticks but the backtick run is followed by more backticks? The greedy `(`{3,})` consumes all of them. Fine.

Now, CRLF: I normalize `\r\n?` → `\n` first. But the spec says "LF 和 CRLF 换行的结果应一致" — results should be consistent. Normalizing first achieves that. But wait — does normalizing affect anything else? `linksFrom` is only used for link extraction; bodies are stored verbatim. So normalizing inside linksFrom is fine.

Hmm, but actually, should I normalize? The fence regex works on lines; if I don't normalize, a closing fence line would end with `\r`, and the info string check `/^[ \t]*$/` would fail because of the `\r`. So yes, normalize.

Now, inline code crossing lines: scanRegion works on the whole region text which may contain `\n`. Backtick runs can span... no wait, backtick runs can't span lines — a run of backticks is consecutive characters; a newline breaks the run. The spec says inline code "可以跨行" (can span lines) — meaning the code span content can include newlines. So `` `a\nb` `` is a code span spanning two lines. My scanner handles that since it scans the whole region text including `\n`. Good.

But wait — "行内代码不能越过围栏代码块边界" — inline code can't cross fence boundaries. Since I split regions at fence lines, an unclosed code span at a region boundary: the closing run search fails within the region → opening treated as literal. That matches the spec.

Hmm, but there's a subtle case: what if a code span is open and the next line is a fence? E.g.:

```
`a
```
[[b]]
```
```

Region 1: "`a\n" — scan: backtick run of length 1, search for closing run of 1 backtick. The region is "`a\n" — no more backticks → close === -1 → opening is literal, continue after it. So `a` is normal text, no links anyway. Then fence, then [[b]] ignored. Good.

Another case:

```
`[[a]]
```
[[b]]
```
```

Region 1: "`[[a]]\n" — backtick run length 1, no closing → literal. Then scan continues from after the opening backtick: `[[a]]` → link `a` found! Per spec: "找不到相同数量的结束反引号时，开头按普通文字处理，后续正常引用仍有效" — yes, `[[a]]` is a valid link. Good, my scanner does that (i = j = 1, then scans `[[a]]` normally).

Now, what about a code span that contains a fence-like line? Can't happen — fences are block-level and code spans are inline; a fence line inside a code span... wait, actually can it? If we're in an inline code span and a line starts with ``` — does the fence open? In CommonMark, fenced code blocks take precedence over inline code: the fence would start a new block. But the spec says "行内代码不能越过围栏代码块边界" — inline code can't cross fence boundaries. So a fence line interrupts the inline code. My region-splitting approach handles this: the fence line is detected regardless of inline code state. Good.

